---
import TileGroup from "../TileGroup.astro";
---
<TileGroup id="projRedis" layout="alternative"  class="my-25">
    <span slot="title" class="text-4xl max-[1050px]:text-2xl font-semibold font-titles"><a href="https://github.com/BHalacsy/Redis-Clone" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="link-text">Redis Clone</a></span>

    <img slot="media" src="/media/icons/skills/redisIcon.svg" alt="Redis Logo" class="w-[50%] h-auto transition-transform duration-300 hover:scale-105">

    <Fragment slot="secondary">
        <span class="text-lg font-medium block mb-1">Tech used</span>
        <p class="text-sm mt-5 font-light text-justify">
            Written in <a href="https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="link-text">C++</a> for high performance using <a href="https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/container" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="link-text">STL containers</a> for efficient in-memory data structures
            without the headache of low level memory management.
            With existing libraries such as <a href="https://www.boost.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="link-text">Boost.Asio</a> for
            asynchronous networking and <a href="https://github.com/uxlfoundation/oneTBB" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="link-text">TBB</a> for fine-grain locking.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm mt-5 font-light text-justify">
            Utilized <a href="https://github.com/catchorg/Catch2" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="link-text">Catch2</a> for unit testing commands and expected behaviour.
            Alongside <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/actions" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="link-text">GitHub Actions</a> for CI.
            Developed and ran on Linux (using <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/wsl/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="link-text">WSL</a>) to have access to <a href="https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/socket.2.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="link-text">sockets</a>.
            Later <a href="https://www.docker.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="link-text">dockerized</a> mainly for the next <a href="#projPortfolio" class="link-text">project</a>.
        </p>
    </Fragment>

    <Fragment slot="description">
        <span class="text-2xl font-semibold">Description</span>
        <p class="text-sm mt-5 font-light text-justify">
            <a href="https://redis.io/docs/latest/develop/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="link-text">Redis</a> is an open-source,
            in-memory key value store database known for its high performance and low latency which supports advanced data types such as sets, lists, hashes and more.
            Using for a variety of things but typically as a cache for large scale databases alongside real-time analytics and <a href="https://redis.io/docs/latest/develop/interact/pubsub/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="link-text">pub/sub messaging</a>.
            For more check out this <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=G1rOthIU-uo" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="link-text">brief summary</a> by Fireship
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm mt-5 font-light text-justify">
            I knew that for my very first independent project I wanted to make some existing piece of software and understand its inner workings.
            Initially I considered following one of <a href="https://codecrafters.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="link-text">CodeCrafters</a>' challenges since they provide a bit more hand holding and have automated tests indicating if you complete a subsection of the challenger,
            however seeing how much it would cost for a subscription, I have opted to instead have no hand holding and do the tests myself.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm mt-5 font-light text-justify">
            I began by deciding what language to write the clone in. <a href="https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="link-text">C++</a> seemed like an ideal choice as I have had plenty of experience using it, and it is closely related to <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/C_(programming_language)" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="link-text">C</a> in which the original Redis is written in.
            Once that was done I made sure to set up a GitHub Actions workflow to run any existing tests automatically on pushes to the repo.
            Having experience with computer networks came in handy since I had to use linux's sockets, encode and parse commands using <a href="https://redis.io/docs/latest/develop/reference/protocol-spec/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="link-text">RESP</a> and have done multithreaded programming multiple times.
            I began by converting existing code (server and client) done in python to C++ which proved to be more of a challenge than I had thought, but once echo message was set up, it was time to get our hands dirty.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm mt-5 font-light text-justify">
            Following this I wanted to follow Redis' <a href="https://redis.io/docs/latest/develop/reference/protocol-spec/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="link-text">RESP protocol</a> for communication as it proved all the utility I had wanted for my clone.
            Initially only allowed to store strings with the basic commands (SET, GET, DEL, EXISTS) I added the first feature of the clone, key expiration.
            I expanded by implementing advanced data structure and their respective commands.
            This had set up the core functionality of the Redis clone, however we were far from done.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm mt-5 font-light text-justify">
            Having had worked with multithreading I decided that a <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thread_pool" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="link-text">thread pool</a> would be fitting for our application to allow concurrent access to the database, this came with its own challenge of handling
            <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Race_condition" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="link-text">race conditions</a> in case multiple threads try to access the same piece of memory. The easy fix for this was to thread lock the kv store in memory when a thread tries to access it, however this
            greatly impacted performance and made accessing the database incredibly slow. I solved this by using TBB's fine-grain locking in which we only lock the currently accessed portion of the kv store
            as opposed to the entire db.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm mt-5 font-light text-justify">
            Another feature I knew I wanted to implement was snapshotting as this allowed for the kv store to remain persistent.
            To do this I utilized <a href="https://www.boost.org/doc/libs/release/libs/serialization/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="link-text">Boost.Serialization</a> which made serializing the kv store much easier as I was using custom data types for managing kv pairs.
            Other features included <a href="https://redis.io/docs/latest/develop/interact/pubsub/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="link-text">Pub/Sub messaging</a>, <a href="https://redis.io/docs/latest/develop/use/pipelining/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="link-text">Pipelining</a>, Transaction support for command queueing and <a href="https://redis.io/docs/latest/develop/reference/eviction/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="link-text">LRU (Least Recently Used) key eviction</a> for memory management.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm mt-5 font-light text-justify">
            Having all these features I had a pretty deep understanding of Redis core functionality, however I always want to make software which has some sort of real world use.
            I decided to dockerize the Redis clone in hopes of running it as a database for a project down the line. As you can imagine I had some ideas, in fact, it runs the very website you are reading this on.
        </p>
        <p class="text-xs mt-5 font-light text-justify">
            For more information check out my GitHub or click the title of this section.
        </p>
    </Fragment>
</TileGroup>
